  "diary": "The air in Lodhi Gardens was clear, but the heat in my hand argued with the register. I walked three kilometres to let the distinction between sign and Notion cool in the body. A name in the estate ledger is a sign; but when the state’s business proceeds on it, the error ceases to be a mere slip of ink. It has become an institutional fact. The misdescription—Hegel made Hegde, a dead professor a Joint Secretary—is not external to the bureaucracy; it is internal to its self-understanding. If the error is only accidental, a correction suffices. But if it has entered the Notion, the contradiction must be worked out in thought before the pen can move. I read §294 of the *Philosophy of Right* twice, seeking the security of the state against the misuse of power. The state protects the officer from private passion, yet the officer’s error becomes the state’s fact. The hierarchy holds, but the sign wavers. This evening, the domestic sign failed too. My ledger said the dhobi comes Saturday for three hundred rupees; Ramesh’s note said Thursday for two hundred. I did not smooth over the discrepancy with household convenience. I asked for Salim’s name and address. State money cannot be settled by memory. It must be traced to the person who made the entry. The house is quiet. The file waits for Monday. The owl spreads its wings only with the falling of dusk, but today, it flew at noon. I found the tension: the state’s sign-system is not repaired by good faith. It is reconciled by administrative consequence. The dead professor is still alive in the ledger, and I must kill him properly.",
